Helios patch 0001 stable-order (Jolt v5.6.0; 02 §7.1, 04 §10.2; WP-0.9, RT-03)

No simulation result may depend on a BodyID (02 §7.1). A BodyID is a slot index that follows each
PhysicsSystem's add and remove history, so a client predictor, the cell and a replay hold the same bodies
under different BodyIDs, and stock Jolt orders solver work by BodyID: a body touching several others solved
its contacts in a different Gauss-Seidel order on each host and diverged in the low bits. This patch orders
by each body's stable key instead: Body::sStableOrderLess compares (object layer, user data), with the
BodyID only as the last tie-break for equal keys. Helios stores the key (EntityId, packed TileKey or PCG
instance key) in the body's user data, and (layer, key) is unique within a PhysicsSystem.

  1. PhysicsSystem::ProcessBodyPair makes the stable-first body "body 1" when both bodies have the same
     motion type (upstream: the lower BodyID), so collision detection runs in the same space everywhere.
  2. ContactConstraintManager stores a pair in stable order (GetContactsFromCache, AddBodyPair,
     AddContactConstraint, OnCCDContactAdded; upstream: lower BodyID first). That order is also each
     contact constraint's body 1 / body 2 and the order ContactListener callbacks see. The caches stay
     keyed by BodyIDs (they are lookups, not orders); WereBodiesInContact, which only has the IDs, looks
     the pair up both ways.
  3. A contact constraint's sort key hashes both bodies' (layer, user data) and sub-shape IDs, instead of
     the cache key's hash of BodyIDs, and SortContacts breaks ties with Body::sStableOrderLess.
  4. CharacterVirtual orders its contacts by B's (object layer, user data) (upstream: BodyID, then the
     CharacterID from a global counter); CharacterContact gains mObjectLayerB for it.
  5. Body::EFlags::NoCrossUpdateCache (bit 7, the last free flag bit; Body::SetNoCrossUpdateCache): on the
     first collision step of each PhysicsSystem::Update, a pair involving a flagged body skips the
     body-pair cache (recomputes its manifold) and starts its impulses at zero; the old manifold is still
     found, so persisted-contact callbacks are unchanged, and later collision steps of the same Update use
     the cache as usual. Helios sets it on ShipHull and Vehicle bodies, whose predicted step must be a
     function of their state: a rollback restores bodies, never the cell's contact cache.

Not covered, because Helios uses neither: the CCD (LinearCast) resolve order (JobResolveCCDContacts sorts
by fraction, then BodyID) and soft bodies. Also not covered: CharacterVirtual's sweeps
(GetFirstContactForSweep) keep the first of several hits at exactly the same fraction, in broadphase
order (engine/physics/README.md lists it for WP-1.5). JPH_CROSS_PLATFORM_DETERMINISTIC and
PhysicsSettings::mDeterministicSimulation stay on; this patch only replaces the order they sort by.

Rebase notes: grep upstream for new BodyID comparisons in Physics/ (`GetID() <`, `mBodyA <`, `mBodyB <`)
and for new users of the ContactConstraintManager cache key order; every pair order must go through
Body::sStableOrderLess or the caches stop finding their entries. If upstream uses flag bit 7, move
NoCrossUpdateCache to a wider flag field rather than dropping it. engine/physics' permuted test build relies
on BodyManager's LIFO free list to reverse every pair's BodyID order (tests/scene.cpp); if that changes,
adapt the test so the order still flips, and check that the tests still fail with this patch reverted.

Upstream status: not submitted. Rebase on every Jolt bump (K10); engine/physics' `determinism:` and
`stable-order:` tests fail if a BodyID order or the cross-Update cache comes back. Licence: MIT (Helios,
like Jolt).

